# Stockhunt repo-root ignores.
#
# The rule of thumb: track code, track findings, ignore anything a machine can
# rebuild. Price caches (1.7 GB) come back from Twelve Data via td_loader.py;
# the bulk sweep intermediates (~400 MB) come back by re-running the sweeps.
# Summaries, meta and curve JSONs ARE tracked -- they are the research record.

# --- environments ---
.venv/
# Per-engine environments for engine-bakeoff/ (see its README to recreate)
.venv-bakeoff/
# The paper trading engine runs on this one (nautilus_trader)
.venv-nautilus/

# --- Claude Code ---
# Session state is not project content, but settings.json IS: it carries the
# deny rules that lock "test research/" and "top 20 stocks/".
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json

# --- credentials, never commit ---
.env.local
*.env.local
# The API's auth store: the allowlist, every live session hash, and the server secret the
# OTP hashes are keyed under. Unlike paper.db this is NOT a record worth keeping in git --
# it is a credential store, and committing it publishes who has access to the desk.
paper api/state/

# --- python ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
scratchpad/
.DS_Store

# --- price data: regenerable, ~1.7 GB ---
/data/
test research/data/
top 20 stocks/data/
backtest master/data/
backtest engine/data/
engine-bakeoff/data_td/
# manifoldbt's Arrow store, rebuilt by run_manifoldbt.py on every run
engine-bakeoff/_mbt_store/
engine-bakeoff/_probe_store/

# --- bulk sweep intermediates: regenerable, ~400 MB ---
# Superseded manual backups from before the itemised fee model.
*.oldfees
# Per-fold and per-asset row dumps. The summaries beside them are tracked.
**/results/*folds*.csv
**/results/*schedule*.csv
**/results/combo_per_asset_*.csv

# --- the paper-trading record ---
# paper.db itself IS tracked: it is the forward-test record and the whole point of the
# store is that it is not lost. Only SQLite's transient sidecars are ignored.
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# --- the results store ---
# The opposite case to paper.db, and the difference is where the truth lives. This one is
# BUILT from the tracked result CSVs by `tools/ingest_results.py` and is regenerable in ten
# seconds, so committing it would put a binary rebuild of already-committed data into every
# diff -- and a stale copy of it would be a second answer to a question the CSVs already
# answer. The submitted-rule rows it holds that the sheets do not are reported by
# `tools/export_results.py`, which is where that gap gets closed deliberately.
walk-forward optimization/results/results.db
**/results/research_book_*.csv
**/results/*.before-export-*.csv

# --- run logs ---
**/logs/
*.log
*.err

# --- generated artifacts ---
# Built by build_report.py from template.html + report.js + the payload.
**/report/index.html
report_payload.json
# Built by the dashboard builder from the results CSVs.
**/web/data.js
**/web/live.json
**/web/paper_curves.json
**/web/curves/
**/dist/dashboard.html

# --- vendored binaries ---
# Cloudflare Tunnel, 52 MB. Download from github.com/cloudflare/cloudflared.
**/tools/cloudflared.exe
.cache/

# Golden-output snapshots. Captured fresh immediately before a refactor and compared
# after; a committed one would silently go stale as the price data changes, which is the
# "stale twin of a generated file" trap this repo already documents for index.html.
tools/golden/

# Per-cell diagnostic dumps. Written for inspection; NOTHING in the repo reads them
# (verified: only `sweep.py` and `combo_sweep.py` write them, no reader anywhere). They
# are now Parquet rather than CSV -- 2.9 GB of these tables became 472 MB -- but even
# compressed they are ~429 MB of regenerable output, so they stay out of git history,
# which is forever. The tables the dashboard DOES read (`wf_per_asset_*`,
# `strat_per_asset_*`) stay tracked, at ~43 MB of Parquet against 317 MB of CSV.
backtest engine/results/per_asset_*.parquet
backtest engine/results/combo_per_asset_*.parquet

# The same rule, applied where it had been missed. Two gaps let 1.5 GB of the identical
# kind of file through: the patterns above are path-anchored, so the `_archive/` copies of
# the very same dumps were still tracked; and `**/results/*folds*.csv` names only CSV, so
# the fold dumps were caught before the Parquet migration and not after. Three of the
# escapees are over GitHub's hard 100 MB per-file limit and cannot be pushed at all.
#
# Regenerable, and read by nothing: `walkforward.py`, `variants.py` and `strat_wf.py`
# write the fold dumps for inspection, and `Stockhunt Dashboard/payload.py` reads
# `edge_standard.csv`, `riskmatch.parquet`, `wf_meta.csv`, `prereg_*`, `parity_live_1d.csv`
# and the curve JSONs -- none of these.
per_asset_*.parquet
combo_per_asset_*.parquet
*folds*.parquet
**/results/_archive/**/per_asset_*.csv
**/results/_archive/**/combo_per_asset_*.csv
**/results/_archive/**/*folds*.csv
**/results/_archive/**/_pre_liquidity_*
**/results/_archive/**/wf_per_asset_*
**/results/_archive/**/strat_per_asset_*
**/results/_archive/**/riskmatch*

# Superseded riskmatch runs. `riskmatch.parquet` itself IS tracked -- `payload.py:462`
# reads it and the dashboard's per-asset table is a groupby over it. These two are the
# pre-fill-fix and mega20-universe copies, which nothing reads.
**/results/riskmatch.closefill_archive.parquet
**/results/riskmatch.mega20.parquet
*.csv.bak
